      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "First, we need to find the missing angle, which we'll call $ x^\\circ $.\n\n[asy]\nsize (5cm,5cm);\npair A,B,C,D,E;\n\nA=(0,1.1);\nB=(4.5,0);\nC=(6.4,1.7);\nD=(4.2,5);\nE=(0.5,4.2);\n\ndraw (A--B--C--D--E--A,linewidth(1));\n\nlabel(\"$P$\",A,SW);\nlabel(\"$x^\\circ$\",shift(-0.3,0.3)*C,NE);\nlabel(\"$128^\\circ$\",shift(0,0.6)*B);\n\nlabel(\"$92^\\circ$\",C,W);\nlabel(\"$113^\\circ$\",shift(-0.3,-0.5)*D);\nlabel(\"$111^\\circ$\",shift(0.5,-0.3)*E);\ndraw(anglemark(B,A,E),blue);\n\n[/asy]\n\nWe can see that $P$ and $128^\\circ$ together form a straight angle, and $92^\\circ$ and $113^\\circ$ together form a straight angle. Therefore, $x^\\circ$ must be the remaining angle, which has measures of $137^\\circ$ (the sum of the two straight angles).\n\n$137^\\circ$ is the measure of angle $P$.",
