    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\) in the given figure, we need to use the property that the sum of the angles in a quadrilateral is \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n\nFirst, let's identify the given angles:\n- Angle \\( A = 128^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( B = 92^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( C = 113^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( D = 111^\\circ \\)\n\nThe angle \\( P \\) is not directly given, but we can deduce its relationship with the other angles. Notice that angles \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are taken from the same sides of the quadrilateral, while angles \\( C \\) and \\( D \\) are taken from the other sides.\n\nSince the sum of the angles in a quadrilateral is \\( 360^\\circ \\), we can write:\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + x = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nwhere \\( x \\) is the measure of angle \\( P \\).\n\nNow, we can calculate the sum of the known angles:\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ = 444^\\circ\n\\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n444^\\circ + x = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = 360^\\circ - 444^\\circ = -84^\\circ\n\\]\nHowever, this negative value indicates that there was a miscalculation. Let's re-evaluate the angles considering the correct order. The angles taken from the same sides of the quadrilateral sum to \\( 213^\\circ \\), and the angles taken from the other sides sum to \\( 157^\\circ + 96^\\circ = 253^\\circ \\).\n\nThus, the correct total for the angles taken from the same sides of the quadrilateral is \\( 213^\\circ \\), and the total for the angles taken from the other sides is \\( 157^\\circ + 96^\\circ = 253^\\circ \\).\n\nLet's correct the calculation:\n\\[\n213^\\circ + 157^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n\\]\nSo, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{21}\n\\]",
